// llmreference: the CPU side of running a Llama-family model: the weight block layouts (GGUF's F32, F16, Q8_0,
// Q4_K and Q6_K), their decoders and the encoders a test needs, seeded synthetic weights, and a CPU twin of each
// GPU kernel in llmkernels (matvec, matmul, RMSNorm, RoPE, softmax, attention over a KV cache, the cache append,
// SiLU, GELU, add, multiply), and a whole model run on the CPU over the twins (the reference llmrun's GPU forward
// pass is checked against). Written in Cone with no GPU and no call of the OS, so it builds and its tests run on
// Linux, where llmkernels (which needs the GPU packages) does not. The gguf package's layouts are the same ones.
//
//   mut rng = random.Rng.fromSeed(7u64);
//   match syntheticMatrix(&mut rng, QTYPE_Q6_K, 4usize, 512usize) {      // 4 rows of 2 Q6_K super-blocks
//     case imm m Some {
//       imm x = syntheticFloats(&mut rng, 512usize, 1.0);
//       mut y = zeroFloats(4usize);
//       matvecCpu(&m, x.view(), y.viewMut());                            // y[r] = row r . x
//       ...
//
// examples/tour.cone is that, whole: it builds a matrix, takes it apart again, and checks one row by hand.
//
//   QTYPE_F32 QTYPE_F16 QTYPE_Q8_0 QTYPE_Q4_K QTYPE_Q6_K     the types, GGUF's ggml_type numbers
//   blockElems(t) blockBytes(t) rowBytes(t, cols)               sizes (0 for an unknown type or a partial block)
//   f16ToF32(h) f32ToF16(f)                                     half-precision conversion, in integers
//   dequantizeRow(t, bytes, at, cols, out)                      one row decoded to f32
//   encodeF32 encodeF16 encodeQ8_0                              bytes of values (Q8_0 as ggml's reference rounds)
//   packWords(bytes)                                            little-endian 32-bit words, for a GPU buffer
//   QMatrix.make(t, rows, cols, bytes)  m.row(r)  m.words()  m.stride()       a weight matrix
//   syntheticMatrix(&mut rng, t, rows, cols)  syntheticFloats(&mut rng, n, amplitude)  zeroFloats(n)
//   matvecCpu matmulCpu rmsnormCpu ropeFrequencies ropeCpu softmaxCpu attentionCpu kvAppendCpu
//   siluCpu geluCpu addCpu mulCpu siluValue geluValue            the twins (each documents its layout)
//   matvecMagnitudeCpu matmulMagnitudeCpu sumTolerance           the bound a sum of products is tested to
//   ModelShape LayerWeights ModelWeights RefCache                 a whole model (model.cone)
//   forwardCpu(&weights, &mut cache, tokens, pos0)                tokens run through every layer: the last one's logits
//   syntheticModel(&mut rng, &shape, &types) isValidWeights(&m)   a seeded random model of chosen weight types
//   embedCpu attentionScale ModelWeights.withShape                the pieces a test uses
//
// METHOD CHOICES. These are defaults a later design may revisit.
//
// - THE LAYOUTS. F32 and F16 are plain little-endian arrays. Q8_0, Q4_K and Q6_K are GGUF's (ggml's) blocks,
//   in llmkernels.cone's header with their sizes and the formula for an element. Their decoders here are
//   written the way ggml's dequantize_row_q8_0, _q4_K and _q6_K are (a loop over blocks and their groups); the
//   shader's (llmcommon.slang) decodes one element, or one unit of 32, at a time, so the tests comparing the
//   two cross-check the layout. UNCERTAIN: no copy of ggml and no model file was to hand, so the Q4_K and Q6_K
//   layouts are written from knowledge of ggml and checked against numbers worked out by hand from it
//   (tests/decode.cone: one block of each type, with Q6_K's values computed by a literal transcription of
//   ggml's loop in Python) and against the gguf package's independent transcription; reading a real model's
//   tensor and comparing it with llama.cpp's is the one check that remains.
// - TWINS ARE REFERENCES. A twin works in f64 where it sums or takes a transcendental, in the order the layout
//   gives, and rounds once to f32; the numbers that DEFINE a result (a dequantised weight, the f32 product
//   that is a RoPE angle, an f32 add) are computed in f32 as the shader does. So a GPU result's difference
//   from its twin is the GPU's own f32 error, which the tests bound (llmkernels.cone's header names each
//   tolerance and why). A twin that emulated the GPU's rounding would be tested against its own mirror.
// - 'sumTolerance(n, absSum)' is (n + 4) * 2^-24 * absSum: the standard bound on an f32 sum of n products, in
//   any order, given the sum of the products' absolute values ('matvecMagnitudeCpu' and 'matmulMagnitudeCpu'
//   compute it for each output).
// - SYNTHETIC WEIGHTS are random blocks (every byte pattern in a nibble, a signed byte or a six-bit scale is a
//   valid value), with the half-precision scales set small enough that decoded weights are O(0.1 .. 1) and
//   a dot product over thousands of them is O(1 .. 10). They are real blocks, not near-misses. The same seed
//   gives the same bytes on every platform (random's generator).
// - THE ENCODERS are the three the tests need. Q8_0 follows ggml's reference rounding (the scale max|v| / 127
//   as a half, each value divided by that half and rounded half away from zero). Left out: encoders for the
//   K-quants (the tests fill blocks with random bytes instead; the gguf package has simple ones), Q4_0, Q5_x,
//   the IQ types and BF16.
// - HALVES are converted by integer arithmetic (no libm, no 16-bit float type): every finite half converts
//   exactly, f32 to half rounds to nearest with ties to even (checked on all 65536 halves and on the midpoint
//   of every adjacent pair), overflow goes to infinity, tiny values to a subnormal half or zero.
// - THE ATTENTION TWIN is one sequence with grouped-query heads and a causal mask, over f32 caches laid out
//   [position][kvHead][d]. Left out, as in the kernel: batching, several sequences, flash attention, sliding
//   windows and biases other than causal, f16 activations or cache.
// - THE FORWARD PASS (model.cone) is the twins in the order of a Llama-family layer, with Qwen3's per-head RMSNorm of
//   the queries and keys when the shape says so, a KV cache, grouped-query heads, tied or separate output matrix,
//   and either rotation order. It is a reference and not fast: every matrix row is decoded to f32 each time it is
//   used. llmrun's GPU pass is checked against it on seeded synthetic models (llmrun's tests/forward.cone names the
//   tolerance and where it comes from). Left out: rope scaling (Llama 3.1, YaRN), sliding windows, a mixture of
//   experts, biases, and any layer type but the one.
// - THE SYNTHETIC MODEL gives each matrix random blocks of its type, with the norm weights set so that activations
//   keep a size where attention is not uniform and a rotation or norm bug moves the logits. A Q4_K block's minimum
//   scale is set to 7.5 of its scale so the weights are zero-mean (random Q4_K blocks are not: with independent
//   random scales and minimums every output of a matrix is nearly the same sum of its input, and attention
//   cannot be told from a mistake in it).
// - No global and no thread-local: every function is of its arguments.

mod llmreference;

import libc;
import collections use List;
import random use Rng;
